The area of magnetic nanocomposites covers a vast variety of different materials and material combinations together with a large field of application, ranging from technical to biomedical applications. The present review is not intended to provide the reader with a complete overview of preparation methods and application scenarios of magnetic nanocomposites. For this purpose, the reader is referred to some of the more recent review articles, covering divers aspects of magnetic nanocomposite materials [3–10]. In this review, a few recent examples have been selected to highlight design concepts and advances for magnetic nanocomposites and magnetic nanoparticles.
## Nanosized particles of magnetic materials
## Introduction
Nanocomposites are multi-phasic materials where the matrix material incorporates units with at least one dimension in the size range below $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . They combine both the properties of the matrix material and the nanosized filler resulting in novel functional materials, which match the needs of a given application. In general, the range of host materials used in nanohybrids is vast, including organic polymers, silica or even liquid media. Host materials, that display tunable properties which respond to an external stimulus, are of particular interest. In this context, the use of magnetic fields as external stimulus has attracted a lot of interest due to several advantages (e.g., the relatively large penetration depth). To produce a response to an external magnetic field, the matrix host is loaded with magnetic nanoparticles, which can be well dispersed in different materials and show
Nanosized particles of ferromagnetic and ferrimagnetic materials reveal interesting magnetic properties depending on the particle size, shape and crystal structure [7]. Nanosized particles of numerous magnetic materials have been incorporated into extended matrix materials (i.e., organic or inorganic polymers) to create integrated functional systems with additional magnetic properties. Due to their unique properties (i.e., magnetic moment in combination with a good chemical stability and low toxicity), magnetite $\mathrm{(Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ and maghemite $(\gamma{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ nanoparticles have attracted much interest for both the fundamental investigations and the various fields of biomedical and technical application $[5,6,11^{\bullet\bullet},4]$ . The iron oxide particles have traditionally been prepared via the co-precipitation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\dot{3}+}$ salts in aqueous, basic media [12]. Although this technique is quite simple, inexpensive and suitable for scale-up, it is often difficult to control the size distribution, crystallinity and magnetization values of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals. Iron oleate appeared as an interesting iron-precursor, which yields crystalline, monodisperse and spherical iron oxide nanoparticles of various sizes in large scale, if it is thermally decomposed in high-boiling, organic solvents [13]. Recently, the phase composition and crystal structure of these iron oxide nanoparticles have been investigated in great detail [14]. The energy of the shape magnetic anisotropy is increasing with increasing morphological anisotropy of the nanoparticles, where the shape anisotropy is zero for spherical nanoparticles and increases with the aspect ratio for nanorods. Recently, substantial progress has been made on the shape-controlled synthesis of magnetic iron oxide nanocrystals, that is, anisotropic, ${\tt\gamma}_{\tt V}{\tt e}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanorods with tunable aspect ratios (Figure 1f) [15]. Spindle-like hematite nanoparticles (i.e., $\mathrm{\bf{\alpha}}_{\mathrm{{\bf{\alpha}}}}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3},$ ) have also gained recent interest due to their weak ferromagnetic properties originating from a canted antiferromagnetism (Figure 1d) [16].
Recently, uniform $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanooctahedra with tunable sizes and size dependent magnetic hyperthermal performance were synthesized by the thermal decomposition of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}$ (Figure 1a) $[17^{\bullet\bullet},23]$ . With these nanooctahedra, specific absorption rates of up to $2629\mathrm{W/g}$ 1 $358\mathrm{kHz}$ , $800\mathrm{Oe})$ were reached. Regular, iron oxide nanocubes were also shown to be very attractive for hyperthermal heating. The $19\mathrm{nm}$ size cubes revealed specific absorption rates of up to $2452\mathrm{W/g}$ 1 $\mathrm{520\kHz}$ , $29\mathrm{kA}/\mathrm{m})$ (Figure 1b) [21]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocubes could also be transferred to the aqueous phase [24].
Spinel-type ferrites (i.e., ${\mathrm{MFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ or ${\mathrm{MO}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}\rangle$ ) can be molecularly engineered to provide a wide range of magnetic properties by replacing the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ by a twovalent metal cation $\mathrm{~\dot{M}~}^{2+}$ (e.g., $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , or $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ). In ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ , the large magnetocrystalline anisotropy and the hard magnetic behavior originate from the coupling between the $\mathrm{\bar{C}o}^{2+}$ and the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions [22]. Various spherical nanoparticles of the spinel-type ferrites have been synthesized using the metal acetylacetonates as precursors [25]. Recently, a general approach for the synthesis of spinel ferrites with cubic shape has been reported, using mixed $\mathrm{M}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ oleate precursors (Figure 1c) $[18^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The growth of spinel ferrite nanocrystals was further investigated in an in situ TEM study [26]. In the hexagonal ferrites of the magnetoplumbit type (e.g., $\mathrm{BaFe}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{19}\rangle$ the oxygen atoms of the spinel-type ferrites are partially substituted by large metal cations (e.g., $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ or $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ), leading to a high intrinsic magnetic anisotropy. Polydisperse nanoplatelets of ${\bf B a F e}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{19}$ , that is, were prepared via the hydrothermal synthesis (Figure 1e) [22].
Due to high values of saturation magnetization, nanoparticles of the ferromagnetic metals (e.g., Fe, Co) also represent attractive constituents for nanocomposites [27]. However, oxidation processes typically result in the rapid degradation of their magnetic properties.
Various strategies for the stabilization of these nanoparticles have been developed (e.g., coating of the metal core with a thin oxidic passivation layer [28,29], with an inert carbon shell (e.g., $\mathrm{Co@C)}$ [30] or with a ferrite layer (e.g., $\mathrm{Fe@MnFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Fe@CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}.$ [31]). Intermetallic systems (e.g., $\mathrm{Nd}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{14}\mathrm{B}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaSmCo_{5}}$ , $\mathrm{Sm}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{17}$ , $\mathrm{L}1_{0}\mathrm{FePt},$ reveal high magnetic anisotropy constants and, thus, many of the magnetically hard nanoparticles are bimetallic alloys, that is FePt [32], $\mathrm{SmCo}_{x}$ [33] and Nd-Fe-B [34] (both prepared via surfactant-assisted ball milling), or MnBi [35].
The properties of magnetic nanoparticles also depend strongly on the presence of interfaces [36]. Core–shelltype nanoparticles of ferromagnetic, ferrimagnetic or antiferromagnetic materials itself represent nanosized composite materials and novel magnetic properties may be obtained as a result of interface engineering between these materials. For example, $\mathrm{FePt}@\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{FePt@Co-}$ 一 $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were shown to exhibit effective exchange coupling phenomena [37]. Recently, the synthesis of exchangecoupled $\mathrm{L}1_{0}–\mathrm{FePd}/\upalpha$ -Fe magnetic nanocomposites from $\mathrm{FePd@Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ core–shell nanoparticles was reported with a tunable coercivity $(\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{c}}0.7–2.4\mathrm{kOe})$ and magnetic saturation $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{s}}$ $\mathrm{(42-140~cmu/g)}$ [38].
Overall, the composition of the implemented magnetic particles as well as their size and shape significantly influence the properties of the nanocomposite and should be well controlled to meet the needs of a given application.
## Functional magnetic nanocomposites with organic matrices
Liquid crystals are highly anistropic organic matrices. Recently, ferromagnetic Sc-doped, single crystal ${\mathrm{BaFe}}_{12}{\mathrm{O}}_{19}$ nanoplatelets were suspended in a thermotropic, nematic liquid crystal (i.e., 4-pentyl- $.4^{\prime}$ -cyanobiphenyl (5CB)), forming a stable, ferromagnetic liquid phase as a result of the balanced magnetic interactions between the platelet-shaped particles and the repulsive forces mediated by the nematic liquid crystal $[39^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The orientational coupling of the nematic director of the liquid crystal and the magnetization of the platelets lead to interesting converse magnetoelectric and (indirect) magneto-optic effects, offering interesting perspectives for future application $[40^{\bullet},41]$ . Due to strong coupling between the orientational order and the mechanical strain, liquid-crystalline elastomers show a remarkable ability to deform significantly at the mesophase-to-isotropic phase transition and are, thus interesting for polymeric shape memory and actuating systems [42]. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles functionalized with 4-(undec-10-enyloxy)benzoic acid were employed as crosslinkers in the formation of such main-chain-type, siloxane-based LC elastomers [43]. To bind the magnetic particles to side-chain-type LC elastomers an additional functionality (e.g., catechol or carboxy groups) was attached to the siloxane backbone for the integration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles [44] or Co nanorods [45].
Hydrogels are of great interest for many applications, including drug delivery, tissue engineering and sensors $[46^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The chemical bonding between the surface of the magnetic nanoparticles and the hydrogel network offers several advantages. It typically allows for a high content in magnetic nanoparticles and avoids the phase separation during the exposure to a magnetic field. Recently, $\mathrm{\alpha}_{\mathrm{\mathbf{{\alpha}}}}\mathrm{\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ spindles have been used as cross-linkers in hybrid particle-linked poly(acrylamide) (PAAm)-based hydrogels (Figure 2) $[47^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The $\mathrm{\alpha}_{\mathrm{\alpha}}\mathrm{e}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ spindles were initially coated with 3-(trimethoxysilyl)propylmethacrylate to covalently bind the particles to the hydrogel matrix and eventually copolymerized with the acrylamide monomer. Soft and hard gels with magnetic and geometric anisotropy were obtained, if the gels were synthesized in the presence of an external homogeneous magnetic field $[19^{\bullet}]$ .
In other work, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanooctahedra could be synthesized in situ in a hydrogel matrix (i.e., poly(2-acrylamido-2- methyl-propane sulfonic acid sodium salt)) [48].
Magnetic nanocomposites based on magnetic nanoparticles (i.e., $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4})$ and organic ionomers represent interesting candidates for self-healing materials $[49^{\bullet}]$ Organic ionomers are typically composed of unpolar polymer backbones and ionic groups, either as part of the polymer side-chains or the backbone. The polar ionic groups form segments, serving as physical cross-linkers. These cross-links were thermally reversible and, thus, the self-healing process could be accelerated via the hyperthermal heating with the magnetic nanoparticles.
The magnetic functionality of a nanocomposite typically scales with its content in magnetic nanoparticles. However, at high particle loadings $(>10\ \mathrm{vol.}\%)$ , particle aggregation may occur due to a low compatibility of the particles with the polymer matrix. Another limitation is the brittleness and the low tensile strength of nanocomposites, often observed at high nanoparticle loadings. Recently, homogeneous magnetic nanocomposites with high nanoparticle content $(77-93~\mathrm{wt.\%})$ , an increased modulus and tensile strength have been prepared from native cellulose, which was extracted from wood $[50^{\bullet}]$ . Thermoplastic biopolymers based on dextrane esters have also been utilized for the synthesis of magnetically responsive composites [51].
Magnetic hyperthermal treatment has attracted much interest for cancer therapy, by inducing a temperature raise in the tumor region to $41\mathrm{-}46~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with an alternating magnetic field to inhibit the regulatory and growth processes of cancer cells. Magnetic nanoparticles have been embedded or conjugated to various polymeric systems, facilitating the magnetically guided targeting of the nanoparticles at the tumor site and/or to the controlled drug release via a deformation or local hyperthermal temperature rise in the polymeric material. For a combined thermotherapeutic and chemotherapeutic treatment, small $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were linked to amine-functionalized poly(ethylene glycol)-poly(lactide) [52]. The as-formed micellar structures $(50-190~\mathrm{nm},$ ) were loaded with the anticancer drug doxorubicin. The authors demonstrated via in vivo cytotoxicity tests, that the combined chemotherapy and hyperthermia in these composite micellar systems induced an enhanced apoptosis of cancer cells.
Ultrasound is a widely applied clinical imaging modality, which provides real time images. Over the last decade, droplets of liquid perfluorocarbons have attracted increasing interest as contrast agents for ultrasound imaging. For the combined magnetically guided drug delivery and ultrasound diagnostics, human serum albumin and poly(ethylene glycol) were used to coat nanodroplets of perfluoropentane, which were loaded with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles and the drug Palitaxel. These hybrids revealed an excellent stability at in vivo temperatures. Besides their potential for ultrasound imaging, the hybrid droplets showed an enhanced extent and rate of apoptosis-inducing cell death, resulting from the synergistic effect of chemotherapy (Palitaxel) and physical vaporization of perfluoropentane.
## Integration of magnetic nanoparticles into inorganic core–shell type nanocomposites
In recent years, the coating of nanosized or microsized magnetic cores with other materials (e.g., $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , carbon, metal oxides, or metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)) to construct core–shell hybrid composites has attracted much interest. In this context, $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ has been a very common material due to its unique properties (i.e., available surface groups for the covalent derivatization, chemical stability, biocompatibility, low toxicity and optical transparency). Recently, magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/{\upgamma}{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ multi core particles were coated with a layer of hierarchically structured $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}\left[53^{\bullet}\right]$ . Initially, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/{\upgamma}{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanocrystallites were coated with a non-porous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ layer in a sol–gel process. In a second step, the sol–gel process was repeated, but, this time, poly(vinylpyrrolidone) was added as a co-template. By pseudomorphic transformation with cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) as a structure directing agent, a hierarchical pore system of worm-like and bubble-like pores was generated in the outer shell of the composite.
Numerous nanocomposites have been designed to exploit the local heating of magnetic nanoparticles in an alternating magnetic field for a phase change of thermoresponsive polymers or cancer treatment. To experimentally study the temperature rise localized in the nanoenvironment of a magnetic core and the heat dissipation, dual-core mesoporous $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ particles were developed (Figure 3a), combining both a magnetic nanoheater 1 $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocrystal) and a temperature-sensitive optical nanothermometer (i.e., $\mathrm{NaYF_{4};Y^{3+},E r^{3+}}$ nanorod) $[54^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . A mesoporous silica shell was selectively grown around the magnetic and the luminescent core in a sol–gel process (Figure 3b). The temperature-dependent intensity ratio of two luminescence bands in the upconversion emission spectrum of the $\mathrm{NaYF}_{4}\mathrm{:Y}^{3+},\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ nanorods was used for the continuous absolute temperature detection in the nanoenvironment around the magnetic material. The interior temperature increase was studied in an alternating magnetic field with variable induction power, exposure time and nanoparticle size. This technique opens the possibility to experimentally quantify the temperature in nanoenvironments and heat transfer at the nanoscale, which is an interesting tool not only for biomedical hyperthermia applications.
Recently, ZnO nanowires were grown around $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ cores to form a chestnut-like hybrid structure and were used as a magnetically recyclable catalyst for the photocatalytic degradation of rhodamine B [55].
MOFs are a very versatile class of three-dimensional networks with high surface area, defined pores and tunable functionalities, offering interesting perspectives for application in various fields (e.g., in gas storage, separations, catalysis, photochemistry, sensing devices and immobilization of proteins) [56]. Recently, the preparation of magnetic core–shell hybrids (i.e., $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF})$ for the specific separation of histidine-rich proteins has been described $[57^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . Core–shell particles of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ (Figure 4a, b) were obtained mixing the ZIF-8 precursors with the multicore $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles. Zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) is a MOF with excellent chemical and thermal stability. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ particles were shown to selectively separate hemoglobin from protein mixtures and human blood samples. In other work, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZnFe_{2}O_{4}}$ hollow nanocomposites with unusual ‘starfish’-like shape were prepared from $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ MOF-5 composites [58].
The large surface area, the acid and base resistance, the high thermal stability (i.e., under inert gas atmosphere) and the abundant functional surface groups, render carbon a very interesting matrix material. An extension of the wellknown Sto¨ber process to the resorcinol–formaldehyde resin (RF) [59], was used to synthesize magnetic core– shell hybrids with a $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ core and a RF polymer layer, or, alternatively, a $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{RF}$ polymer shell $[60^{\bullet}]$ . Eventually, the organic polymer layer was thermally transformed into a carbon shell under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere (Figure 4c, d).
## Conclusions
In the last two years, there have been many exciting developments in the use of magnetic nanoparticles for magnetic composite materials of various types. In this review, recent advances in magnetic nanocomposites have been addressed and illustrated by few selected samples. Recently, nanoparticles of iron oxide and other interesting magnetic materials with cubic, cuboctahedral, or rod-like shape have been made available, exhibiting interesting properties (e.g., geometric shape anisotropy, large magnetic moments, excellent heating properties, high magnetic anisotropy). These particles provide unexplored perspectives for the synthesis of nanocomposite materials with novel or enhanced properties. Various exciting, multi-responsive systems have been prepared by integrating magnetic nanoparticles in various thermoresponsive polymers, liquid crystals, or mechanically deformable elastomers. Magnetic nanocomposite materials derived from renewable resources will certainly be of increasing future importance. Inorganic or metal organic materials (e.g., $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , MOFs, carbon) have been grown in situ on magnetic cores, revealing core–shell-type and other unusual particle morphologies. Shell materials with high and adjustable porosity (e.g. MOFs, hierarchical pore systems) are particularly interesting for separations and catalysis. New combinations of magnetic nanoparticles and host materials still offer many unexplored perspectives for applications in diverse fields, ranging from biomedical to technical applications.
